KaiT2en-NixOS divergence: `t2airshare daemon` takes --port.

The receiver binds port 0, so the kernel hands it a different ephemeral
port on every start (40477, 34929, 34867, 35721, 44175 over one
debugging session). The port is in the mDNS SRV record, so Quick Share
senders always find it, but NixOS's firewall is default-deny and
`networking.firewall.allowedTCPPorts` cannot name a port that moves.
Fedora never needed this: the FedoraWorkstation firewalld zone leaves
the high ports open, so upstream has nothing to declare.

Worse than per-boot: the app's own receive toggle runs `systemctl --user
enable|disable` on t2-airshare.service, so every toggle re-rolls the
port and invalidates any rule naming the old one.

`receive` already has exactly this flag (`--port`, default 0); this
gives `daemon` the same one and threads it into service::run, which
keeps binding 0 when it is unset. nix/modules/t2-airshare passes
`--port` and opens that one port.

Applied by apps/t2-airshare/package.nix on top of the upstream source
(-p1 relative to apps/t2-airshare).

Drop condition: remove when upstream's daemon takes a fixed port (or
the receiver binds a well-known one), and let the module open that
instead. Re-check every sync.

